# 04 — Obfuscation, Scoring & Event-Day Infrastructure

## Web path & flag obfuscation

Because this base VM has existing public write-ups, leaving default paths
and flag formats in place would let anyone search their way to the answers
instead of solving the challenge. Three things were changed:

**1. Renamed the predictable default directories:**

| Original | Renamed to |
|---|---|
| `/flag/` | `/hidden_flag_dir/` |
| `/admin_area/` | `/portal_secure_99/` |
| `/uploaded_files/` | `/uos_uploads/` |

The application's own PHP source was updated to point at the new upload
path, and `robots.txt` was rewritten to disclose the *new* paths (plus decoy
entries) so the recon step still worked as intended, just against paths a
public write-up wouldn't reference.

**2. Reformatted every flag** from the original's plain numeric-string format
to a unified, event-branded format (`{EVENTNAME_FLAGN_XXXXXX}`), so a
participant can't simply paste a number from an old write-up and have it
match.

**3. Shifted the web service off its default port** (80 → 8080), so a
top-1000-ports scan misses it entirely and a full `nmap -p-` is required —
rewarding thorough reconnaissance rather than a default scan.

```bash
sed -i 's/Listen 80/Listen 8080/g' /etc/apache2/ports.conf
sed -i 's/<VirtualHost \*:80>/<VirtualHost \*:8080>/g' /etc/apache2/sites-enabled/000-default.conf
service apache2 restart
```

## QA process (what actually went wrong, and how it was caught)

A "blind test" pass — re-solving the entire machine end-to-end after
customization, exactly as a participant would — caught three real bugs
that a code review alone would have missed:

- **A silent, partial find/replace failure.** One flag's replacement command
  matched and worked; an identical-looking command for a different flag
  silently matched nothing (a copy-paste encoding mismatch), leaving the
  *original* flag value live. Caught only by manually reading the flag file
  back after the "fix," not by assuming the command had succeeded.
- **A typo in the final flag's text**, caught the same way.
- **An over-corrected permission change.** One flag file being
  world-readable initially looked like an unintended information leak, and
  was locked down — which broke the actual intended discovery path, since
  that file was *supposed* to be reachable by a low-privileged web shell as
  part of the enumeration chain. Reverted after tracing the intended solve
  path back through the challenge design.

**Lesson:** verifying a fix by re-reading the actual target state (not by
assuming a command that returned no error also did what it was supposed to)
caught real, event-blocking issues before they reached participants.

## CTFd integration

- 5 challenges, chained via CTFd's per-challenge "requirements" so each flag
  only becomes visible after the previous one is solved
- Scoring: 50 / 100 / 150 / 200 / 250 = 750 points total, across Enumeration,
  Web Exploitation, and Privilege Escalation categories
- Verified with a full non-admin test account run before going live —
  progressive unlock and scoring confirmed working end-to-end

## Event-day network architecture

With institutional hosting unavailable, the deployment model was:

- **Per-participant isolation:** each participant's target VM and attacker
  (Kali) VM ran side-by-side on their *own* laptop, connected only to each
  other via a VirtualBox Host-Only network — never exposed to any other
  participant's machine, and never handed over as a file
- **Centralized live scoreboard:** CTFd ran on the organizer's laptop,
  reachable separately over a dedicated mobile hotspot from each
  participant's own browser (kept deliberately separate from the isolated
  per-laptop attack traffic)
- **Supervised deployment, not file distribution:** every target VM instance
  was imported and powered on by the organizer directly, with participants
  never given the VM file itself — closing off offline attack vectors
  (disk-mounting the VM image, GRUB recovery-mode root access) that would
  otherwise bypass the entire challenge

This scaled to roughly 10 simultaneous participants on a single
organizer-hosted hotspot.